create_model() calls strtod() on a model-card word that is not a known
parameter, to tell a stray number from an unknown parameter name. With
musl (Emscripten), strtod() sets errno to EINVAL when it converts nothing,
so an unknown name (as in the sky130 resistor models) became a fatal
"strtod: Invalid argument" instead of the warning "unrecognized parameter
... ignored". glibc leaves errno alone in that case. Only an out-of-range
number is an error.

--- a/src/spicelib/parser/inpgmod.c	2026-11-06 19:28:02.563294913 +0000
+++ b/src/spicelib/parser/inpgmod.c	2026-11-06 19:28:02.565331969 +0000
@@ -180,7 +180,9 @@
                 errno = 0;    /* To distinguish success/failure after call */
                 dval = strtod(parm, &endptr);
                 /* Check for various possible errors */
-                if ((errno == ERANGE && dval == HUGE_VAL) || errno != 0) {
+                /* only a value out of range is an error: when nothing is
+                   converted, C libraries may set errno (musl sets EINVAL) */
+                if (endptr != parm && errno == ERANGE && dval == HUGE_VAL) {
                     perror("strtod");
                     controlled_exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
                 }
